$DIR/main.p:13:2: onPanic is the panic handler, so it must not wait
    A panic can happen anywhere: in an interrupt handler, or while the code that
    panicked holds a lock. The panic handler runs on top of that code, so if it
    waits for a lock or a channel, it may wait for code that can never run
    again, and the program hangs instead of reporting the panic.
    onPanic calls os.Stderr.WriteString (main.p:13), which calls f.Write
    (os_linux.p:220), which calls f.write (os_linux.p:177), which calls f.waitIO
    (os_linux.p:197), which calls f.poll.Wait (os_linux.p:85), which may be
    rt.(*PollDesc).Wait, which may wait.
    f.poll.Wait calls through an interface, so it counts as calling the Wait
    method of every type the program converts to os.ioWaiter.
    The path goes through an os.File. Writing to a file waits for the poller
    when the file is a pipe or a socket that is full, and the check cannot tell
    which file a call writes to. rt.Console writes to standard error with a
    system call that never waits.
    A kernel that waits in an interrupt handler hangs, often only under load and
    far from the cause, so Plutonium checks the whole program at compile time
    instead.
    Write to rt.Console{} instead of os.Stderr in a panic or interrupt handler;
    os.File writes may wait for the poller:
        p.Print(rt.Console{})
    (see "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.6)
    (run "pluto explain E2584" for a longer explanation)
